BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:queer-art-design-grant-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260823T184308Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260814
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260815
SUMMARY:Deadline: Queer|Art Design Grant 2026 (Inaugural)
DESCRIPTION:The Queer|Art Design Grant is a new initiative from Queer|Art s
 upporting projects that explore queer histories\, legacies\, and futures w
 hile providing visibility and opportunity for emerging LGBTQ+ design talen
 t. AWARDS: two USD 10\,000 grants to emerging queer creatives across the d
 esign industry\, described as the largest cash grant for LGBTQ+ designers 
 in the country. ELIGIBILITY: US-based designers working across a variety o
 f disciplines\, including graphic design\, architecture and landscape arch
 itecture\, industrial and product design\, fashion\, textile and wearables
 \, digital design\, service design\, furniture and home decor\, materials 
 research\, interior design\, and more. In its inaugural year the grant is 
 judged by a panel of designers\, curators\, architects\, and cultural lead
 ers\, including Bex McCharen\, Salome Asega\, Cristina Grajales\, Jaffer K
 olb\, and Paul Soulellis. The program is the first major initiative under 
 a new partnership between UGG and Queer|Art\; Queer|Art board member and c
 reative strategist Fran Tirado serves as Community Curator. Winners will b
 e announced at Queer|Art's Annual Party in Fall 2026. DEADLINE: 14 August 
 2026. Apply via https://www.queer-art.org/design-grant.\n\nOrganisation: Q
 ueer|Art (in partnership with UGG)\nLocation: United States\; open to US-b
 ased emerging queer/LGBTQ+ designers.\nAward: Two USD 10\,000 grants (desc
 ribed as the largest cash grant for LGBTQ+ designers in the US). No applic
 ation fee.\nDuration: One-off grant\; winners announced at Queer|Art's Ann
 ual Party\, Fall 2026\n\nMore: https://www.queer-art.org/design-grant?utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.queer-art.org/design-grant?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United States\; open to US-based emerging queer/LGBTQ+ designers.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:van-alen-open-access-exhibition-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260823T184308Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260628
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260629
SUMMARY:Deadline: Van Alen Institute: Open Access - Open Call for Exhibitio
 n Proposals 2026
DESCRIPTION:Open Access: Exploring 130 Years of American Design is a reques
 t for exhibition proposals that treats the Van Alen Institute archive (tho
 usands of competition boards\, jury records\, photographs and corresponden
 ce) as living material to be questioned and reinterpreted through the lens
  of open and fair access. Van Alen invites emerging designers and creative
 s of all disciplines to engage the archive\, either responding directly to
  specific materials (competition prompts\, submitted drawings) or thematic
 ally. A wide range of media is welcomed\, including architectural models\,
  drawings\, images\, projections\, video\, photography and writing\; direc
 t engagement\, interaction and multi-disciplinary collaboration are encour
 aged. Five projects will be selected and shown together at Van Alen Instit
 ute\, 28 September to 13 November 2026. ELIGIBILITY: open to emerging crea
 tives across disciplines (architects\, artists\, designers\, filmmakers\, 
 photographers\, researchers\, writers and others)\; no formal architectura
 l training required. Applicants must be at least 18\; be legally authorize
 d to work in the United States OR capable of receiving an artist honorariu
 m (so non-US applicants able to receive an honorarium may apply\, though t
 he Brooklyn exhibition and hybrid check-ins make it US-practical)\; be ava
 ilable for hybrid check-ins July to November 2026\; and commit to deliveri
 ng exhibition-ready work on the project timeline. Collaborative proposals 
 are permitted\, though the honorarium may be shared among collaborators. S
 elected participants receive a USD 3\,000 honorarium\, archival access and
  curatorial support\, mentorship from Van Alen staff and advisors\, free m
 aterials through Materials for the Arts\, and professional documentation. 
 Supported by the National Endowment for the Arts and the New York State Co
 uncil on the Arts. Application deadline: 28 June 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Va
 n Alen Institute\nLocation: Brooklyn\, New York\, USA. Exhibition at Van A
 len Institute\, 28 September to 13 November 2026\nAward: USD 3\,000 honora
 rium\, plus archival access\, curatorial support\, mentorship\, free mater
 ials via Materials for the Arts\, professional documentation\, and inclusi
 on in the public exhibition. Five projects selected. No application fee.\n
 Duration: Hybrid project check-ins July to November 2026\; exhibition 28 S
 eptember to 13 November 2026\n\nMore: https://www.vanalen.org/project/open
 -access/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.vanalen.org/project/open-access/?utm_source=artificialnouve
 augrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Brooklyn\, New York\, USA. Exhibition at Van Alen Institute\, 28 S
 eptember to 13 November 2026
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:new-inc-year-13-mentor-open-call-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260823T184308Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260708
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260709
SUMMARY:Deadline: NEW INC Year 13 Mentor Open Call
DESCRIPTION:NEW INC\, the New Museum's cultural incubator\, is seeking ment
 ors for Year 13 of its program supporting creative practitioners at the in
 tersection of art\, design\, and technology. This is a call for mentors (c
 reative and business leaders)\, not for program members. TWO MENTOR TYPES:
  Dedicated Mentors ($500 honorarium) are matched one-on-one with a member 
 and commit to ongoing support throughout the year\; Office Hours Mentors (
 $300 honorarium) offer one-on-one sessions to the broader community on an 
 as-needed basis. All mentors receive a one-year All Access Plus membership
  to the New Museum\, invitations to special events including the annual DE
 MO Festival\, and access to community-only workshops\, talks\, and social 
 events. YEAR 13 PROGRAM TRACKS mentors may support: Art & Code\; Creative 
 Science\; Extended Realities\; Launchpad\; Social Architecture. KEY DATES:
  Mentor Open Call closes 8 July 2026\; Mentor Open House (virtual info ses
 sion) 17 June 2026\, 12-1pm ET (register via the site)\; mandatory Dedicat
 ed Mentor matchmaking ranking session 11 September 2026\; mandatory Dedica
 ted Mentor matchmaking event 18 September 2026\; Dedicated Mentor matches 
 announced 2 October 2026. Most NEW INC activities take place Monday-Friday
 \, 10am-6pm ET. Apply at https://www.newmuseum.org/new-inc/open-call/.\n\n
 Organisation: NEW INC (New Museum Incubator)\nLocation: New York\, NY\, US
 A\; primarily in-person at 250 Hudson St\, lower Manhattan. Some hybrid pa
 rticipation possible.\nAward: Dedicated Mentors: $500 honorarium. Office H
 ours Mentors: $300 honorarium. All mentors also receive a one-year All Acc
 ess Plus membership to the New Museum. No application fee.\nDuration: Year
  13 program (September 2026 onward)\; average 4-8 hours of engagement per 
 week.\n\nMore: https://www.newmuseum.org/new-inc/open-call/?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.newmuseum.org/new-inc/open-call/?utm_source=artificialnouve
 augrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:New York\, NY\, USA\; primarily in-person at 250 Hudson St\, lower
  Manhattan. Some hybrid participation possible.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:new-public-entrepreneur-in-residence-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260823T184308Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260909
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260910
SUMMARY:Deadline: New_ Public Entrepreneur in Residence\, Pilot Cohort
DESCRIPTION:New_ Public is piloting a virtual Entrepreneur in Residence pro
 gramme for solo founders building alternatives to the mainstream social pl
 atforms. Its framing: people are turning away from algorithmic social medi
 a\, AI bots are taking over the big platforms\, and users are retreating i
 nto private closed spaces\, which leaves an opening for builders working o
 n a new information ecosystem rather than the status quo. The residency su
 pplies research\, experts\, connections and tools\; the founder brings the
  vision and experience. TWO FOCUS AREAS: humans connecting to humans\, mea
 ning products that bring people into meaningful contact online (forums\, m
 essaging apps\, groups\, microblogging\, in-person meetups)\, including AI
 -powered platforms and tools as long as they connect people to people rath
 er than people to bots\; and local and civic information environments\, me
 aning tools for distributing local news\, building community knowledge and
  facilitating collective sensemaking\, so people can get trustworthy infor
 mation about their communities. WHAT RESIDENTS DO: use self-directed time 
 to prototype ideas\, test assumptions and gain clarity\; participate in re
 gular check-ins\, engage with the other resident and the wider team\, and 
 take candid feedback on product and strategy\; engage seriously with go-to
 -market questions about who the user is\, how they find you\, and the path
  to sustainability\; and help shape the programme itself\, since the pilot
  cohort's feedback directly informs how the EIR programme runs going forwa
 rd. WHO THEY WANT: entrepreneurial founders ready to build their next thin
 g within the focus areas\, with a track record of or clear drive toward wo
 rk that builds social trust\, connection and belonging\; strong product th
 inkers with good judgment\, a relentless focus on user needs\, and opennes
 s to coaching on mission-aligned business models. An early idea is enough\
 , no working product required. The pilot is open to founders with somethin
 g else going on alongside\, though the full programme will most likely be 
 full time. APPLICATION PROCESS: submit the written application online\; sh
 ortlisted applicants submit a short video by 18 September 2026 covering ba
 ckground\, motivation and a quick pitch\; full applications and videos go 
 to the advisory board\, with offers made in early October ahead of the kic
 koff. FUTURE COHORTS: New_ Public plans to open the next cohort to the UK 
 and EU from spring 2027. Deadline 11:59pm Pacific on Wednesday 9 September
  2026.\n\nOrganisation: New_ Public\nLocation: Remote\, with an in-person 
 kickoff retreat in Philadelphia\, USA (19 to 22 October 2026). Open to app
 licants based in the United States or Canada.\nAward: Stipend of USD 22\,5
 00\, paid in three monthly installments across the residency\, each contin
 gent on continued active participation. Plus USD 1\,500 toward an AI tool 
 of the resident's choice for coding\, prototyping or design. Two residents
  are selected in the pilot cohort. Also provided: hands-on product and str
 ategy support from senior New_ Public staff and outside experts\; access t
 o New_ Public's five years of original research on moderation\, governance
 \, community stewardship and platform design\, plus direct access to the r
 esearchers behind it\; and introductions across New_ Public's network of m
 ajor platforms\, startups\, university researchers and funders for hiring\
 , fundraising\, early user testing and community partnerships. Residents m
 ust submit a Form W-9 or W-8BEN to receive the stipend. No application fee
 .\nDuration: Three months\, from the week of 19 October 2026 to 29 January
  2027\, run remotely apart from the opening retreat\n\nMore: https://newpu
 blic.org/EIR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://newpublic.org/EIR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, with an in-person kickoff retreat in Philadelphia\, USA (
 19 to 22 October 2026). Open to applicants based in the United States or C
 anada.
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
